---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Header from "../components/Header.astro";
import Footer from "../components/Footer.astro";

const emailSubject = "mailto:olga@okad.agency?subject=UGC%20Repurposing%20%E2%80%94%20tell%20us%20about%20your%20brand";
const calHref = "https://calendly.com/okad_calls/first";
---

<Layout
  title="How to Repurpose UGC Across Channels: Maximize Every Video | OKAD"
  description="Turn one UGC video into 10+ assets across TikTok, Meta, YouTube, email, and product pages. Repurposing frameworks, editing tips, and platform-specific adaptations. From an agency with 10,000+ ads."
>
  <Header />
  <main>

    <!-- Hero -->
    <section class="pt-20 md:pt-24 pb-10 text-center bg-bg">
      <div class="max-w-[800px] mx-auto px-6">
        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 text-[12px] font-medium text-accent bg-[rgba(99,102,241,.06)] border border-[rgba(99,102,241,.18)] rounded-full px-3.5 py-1.5 mb-6">
          Content strategy &middot; September 2026
        </div>
        <h1 class="font-display font-bold text-[clamp(28px,5vw,48px)] leading-[1.1] tracking-[-0.03em] text-ink mb-4">
          How to Repurpose UGC <span class="text-accent">Across Every Channel</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-[18px] text-ink-2 max-w-[600px] mx-auto leading-[1.6] mb-8">
          One UGC video can become ten assets &mdash; paid ads, email headers, product-page testimonials, LinkedIn clips, YouTube Shorts, and more. Most brands use a video once and move on. The smart ones repurpose everything.
        </p>
        <div class="flex gap-3 justify-center flex-wrap mb-8">
          <a href={emailSubject} class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-6 py-3 rounded-xl font-sans text-[14px] font-semibold bg-accent text-white hover:bg-accent-hover transition-all no-underline">Get a repurposing plan &rarr;</a>
          <a href={calHref} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-6 py-3 rounded-xl font-sans text-[14px] font-semibold bg-card text-ink border border-border hover:border-accent hover:text-accent transition-all no-underline">Book a call &rarr;</a>
        </div>
        <div class="flex gap-5 flex-wrap justify-center font-mono text-[12px] text-muted">
          <span>1 video &rarr; 10+ assets</span>
          <span>6 platforms</span>
          <span>10,000+ ads produced</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Why Repurposing Wins grid -->
    <section class="py-8">
      <div class="max-w-[800px] mx-auto px-6">
        <h2 class="font-display text-[22px] font-bold tracking-[-0.02em] text-center mb-5">Why Repurposing Wins</h2>
        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-3 gap-3.5">
          <div class="bg-card border border-border rounded-[10px] p-4 text-center">
            <div class="text-[22px] mb-1.5">&#x1F4B0;</div>
            <h3 class="font-display text-[14px] font-semibold mb-1">Lower Cost per Asset</h3>
            <p class="text-[12px] text-muted leading-[1.4]">Production costs stay flat while asset count multiplies. A $500 video becomes $50 per derivative when you cut it into ten formats.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-card border border-border rounded-[10px] p-4 text-center">
            <div class="text-[22px] mb-1.5">&#x1F4CA;</div>
            <h3 class="font-display text-[14px] font-semibold mb-1">More Test Surface</h3>
            <p class="text-[12px] text-muted leading-[1.4]">Every derivative is a new test. Different hooks, lengths, and formats reveal what your audience responds to &mdash; without shooting new footage.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-card border border-border rounded-[10px] p-4 text-center">
            <div class="text-[22px] mb-1.5">&#x26A1;</div>
            <h3 class="font-display text-[14px] font-semibold mb-1">Faster Velocity</h3>
            <p class="text-[12px] text-muted leading-[1.4]">Repurposing fills your content calendar without waiting for new creator shoots. Ship ten assets in a day instead of one per week.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- TOC -->
    <section class="py-10 border-t border-b border-border">
      <div class="max-w-[800px] mx-auto px-6">
        <div class="font-mono text-[11px] font-medium uppercase tracking-[.1em] text-muted-2 mb-4">Contents</div>
        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-1.5">
          {[
            { num: "01", text: "Why Repurposing Is the Highest-ROI Strategy", href: "#why" },
            { num: "02", text: "The Framework: One Video, Ten Assets", href: "#framework" },
            { num: "03", text: "Platform-Specific Adaptations", href: "#platforms" },
            { num: "04", text: "Reformatting for Email and SMS", href: "#email-sms" },
            { num: "05", text: "UGC on Product Pages and Landing Pages", href: "#product-pages" },
            { num: "06", text: "Editing Techniques for Repurposed Content", href: "#editing" },
            { num: "07", text: "Rights and Usage: What Your Contract Needs", href: "#rights" },
            { num: "08", text: "Building a Repurposing Workflow", href: "#workflow" },
            { num: "09", text: "FAQ", href: "#faq" },
          ].map((item) => (
            <a href={item.href} class="flex items-center gap-2.5 px-3 py-2 rounded-lg text-[14px] text-ink-2 font-medium hover:bg-bg-alt transition-colors no-underline">
              <span class="font-mono text-[11px] font-medium text-accent min-w-[22px]">{item.num}</span>
              {item.text}
            </a>
          ))}
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 01 Why Repurposing -->
    <section class="guide-section" id="why">
      <div class="max-w-[800px] mx-auto px-6">
        <div class="font-mono text-[11px] font-medium uppercase tracking-[.1em] text-accent mb-2">01 &mdash; Strategy</div>
        <h2 class="font-display font-bold text-[clamp(24px,3.5vw,32px)] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink mb-2">Why repurposing is the highest-ROI UGC strategy</h2>
        <div class="tldr">
          <ul class="list-none p-0 m-0 space-y-1">
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">One video can yield 10&ndash;15 derivative assets at zero additional production cost</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">More creative variants = more testing = faster learning on what converts</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">The alternative &mdash; shooting fresh content for every channel &mdash; does not scale</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">Most brands treat a UGC video as a single-use asset. A creator delivers a 30-second testimonial, the media buyer runs it as one ad on Meta, and that is the end of its life. The same video could have been cut into a 15-second hook test, a 6-second bumper, a static thumbnail with a pull quote, an email GIF, a product-page testimonial clip, and a LinkedIn thought-leadership snippet. That is seven assets from one shoot &mdash; and we have not even touched aspect-ratio variations.</p>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">The math is straightforward. If a single UGC video costs $400 to produce and you use it once, your cost per asset is $400. If you repurpose it into ten derivatives, your cost per asset drops to $40 &mdash; and the editing time is a fraction of what a new shoot would require. This is why repurposing is the highest-leverage activity in any creative operation.</p>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">There is a compounding benefit, too. Each derivative is a new creative variant you can test in your ad account. Different hooks, different lengths, different opening frames &mdash; all from the same source footage. Brands that repurpose aggressively find winning creatives faster because they have more variants in rotation. The ones that shoot-and-forget are always waiting for the next batch of content.</p>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">The operational advantage is just as important. When your content team is waiting on new creator deliveries, your ad account goes stale. <a href="/ad-fatigue" class="text-accent hover:underline">Ad fatigue</a> sets in, CPAs climb, and the media buyer has nothing new to test. A repurposing pipeline solves this by generating fresh creative variants from existing footage &mdash; buying time between shoots without sacrificing testing velocity.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 02 Framework -->
    <section class="guide-section" id="framework">
      <div class="max-w-[800px] mx-auto px-6">
        <div class="font-mono text-[11px] font-medium uppercase tracking-[.1em] text-accent mb-2">02 &mdash; Framework</div>
        <h2 class="font-display font-bold text-[clamp(24px,3.5vw,32px)] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink mb-2">The repurposing framework: one video, ten assets</h2>
        <div class="tldr">
          <ul class="list-none p-0 m-0 space-y-1">
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Start with the longest, highest-quality version &mdash; the &ldquo;master asset&rdquo;</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Cut down, reformat, and recombine rather than shooting new footage</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Each derivative targets a specific channel, format, and viewer intent</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">The framework starts with what we call the <strong>master asset</strong> &mdash; the full-length, highest-resolution version of the UGC video. Typically 30&ndash;90 seconds, vertical (9:16), with clean audio. Everything else is derived from this single source file.</p>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">The ten derivatives</h3>
        <ol class="list-none p-0 counter-reset-step space-y-0">
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative border-b border-border text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Full-length ad (30&ndash;60s)</strong> &mdash; the master asset with brand intro/outro and captions. Primary ad unit for Meta and TikTok.</li>
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative border-b border-border text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Hook test cuts (5&ndash;8s)</strong> &mdash; three to five alternate openings spliced onto the same body. Tests which first three seconds drive the highest <a href="/hook-rate" class="text-accent hover:underline">hook rate</a>.</li>
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative border-b border-border text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Short-form vertical (15s)</strong> &mdash; tightest edit of the core message. For TikTok, Reels, and YouTube Shorts where brevity wins.</li>
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative border-b border-border text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Square crop (1:1)</strong> &mdash; center-cropped for Facebook Feed and Instagram Feed placements where square outperforms vertical.</li>
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative border-b border-border text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Landscape crop (16:9)</strong> &mdash; for YouTube pre-roll, LinkedIn video ads, and desktop landing pages.</li>
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative border-b border-border text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Static thumbnail</strong> &mdash; a high-impact frame grab with the creator's face and a pull-quote overlay. Works as a display ad, email header, or social image.</li>
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative border-b border-border text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>GIF/cinemagraph (3&ndash;5s loop)</strong> &mdash; looping clip of the most expressive moment. For email modules, product pages, and retargeting banners.</li>
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative border-b border-border text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Audio-only testimonial</strong> &mdash; extracted audio for podcast ads, Spotify placements, or voiceover layered onto b-roll.</li>
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative border-b border-border text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Pull-quote card</strong> &mdash; the strongest one-liner from the creator set over a branded background. For Stories, carousel slides, and social proof sections.</li>
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Compilation/mashup</strong> &mdash; fragments from multiple creators stitched into a single &ldquo;wall of voices&rdquo; ad. Powerful for <a href="/social-proof-ads" class="text-accent hover:underline">social proof</a> campaigns.</li>
        </ol>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4 mt-6">Not every video will yield all ten. Some footage does not crop well to landscape; some creators speak too softly for audio-only extraction. The point is to evaluate every master asset against this list and extract as many viable derivatives as the source allows.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 03 Platform-Specific Adaptations -->
    <section class="guide-section" id="platforms">
      <div class="max-w-[800px] mx-auto px-6">
        <div class="font-mono text-[11px] font-medium uppercase tracking-[.1em] text-accent mb-2">03 &mdash; Platforms</div>
        <h2 class="font-display font-bold text-[clamp(24px,3.5vw,32px)] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink mb-2">Platform-specific adaptations</h2>
        <div class="tldr">
          <ul class="list-none p-0 m-0 space-y-1">
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Same content, different specs: aspect ratio, length, captions, safe zones</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">TikTok rewards raw energy; Meta rewards clarity; YouTube rewards depth</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">LinkedIn requires a professional reframe &mdash; not just a re-upload</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">TikTok</h3>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">Vertical 9:16, 15&ndash;60 seconds. TikTok's algorithm rewards watch-through rate, so the edit needs to be tight &mdash; no slow intros, no dead space. Captions are expected (70%+ of viewers watch with sound off at least some of the time). Use <a href="/spark-ads" class="text-accent hover:underline">Spark Ads</a> to run repurposed content from the creator's account for maximum native feel. Keep text and CTAs inside the safe zone (top 15% and bottom 20% are covered by UI elements).</p>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">TikTok also favors a raw, unpolished aesthetic. If your master asset was edited with branded lower-thirds and polished transitions, consider stripping those for the TikTok derivative. The platform's native content looks casual &mdash; your repurposed ads should match that energy. Trending sounds can be added to silent or low-audio clips to boost discoverability in organic placements.</p>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">Meta (Instagram &amp; Facebook)</h3>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">Reels: 9:16, up to 90 seconds. Feed: 1:1 or 4:5 performs best. Stories: 9:16 with a front-loaded message (viewers tap through quickly). For <a href="/ugc-whitelisting" class="text-accent hover:underline">whitelisted campaigns</a>, run the creator's content as Partnership Ads. Facebook Feed still favors square video for in-feed engagement. Always export separate cuts for Reels and Feed &mdash; the same file rarely works for both placements.</p>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">YouTube</h3>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">Shorts: 9:16, under 60 seconds &mdash; essentially the same cut as TikTok, but YouTube's audience skews slightly older, so a calmer pacing can work. Pre-roll/in-stream: 16:9, 15&ndash;30 seconds. The landscape crop of your master asset works here. Add a clear CTA card in the final 5 seconds. YouTube is the only major platform where landscape video still outperforms vertical in ad placements.</p>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">LinkedIn</h3>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">1:1 or 16:9, 30&ndash;90 seconds. LinkedIn requires a fundamentally different framing. A TikTok-style testimonial feels out of place here. Instead, reframe the UGC as a case study or a lesson: &ldquo;Here&rsquo;s what happened when we tested creator-led content for [brand].&rdquo; Add a text intro, data overlays, and a professional-tone CTA. The raw footage stays the same &mdash; the context around it changes.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 04 Email and SMS -->
    <section class="guide-section" id="email-sms">
      <div class="max-w-[800px] mx-auto px-6">
        <div class="font-mono text-[11px] font-medium uppercase tracking-[.1em] text-accent mb-2">04 &mdash; Email &amp; SMS</div>
        <h2 class="font-display font-bold text-[clamp(24px,3.5vw,32px)] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink mb-2">Reformatting for email and SMS</h2>
        <div class="tldr">
          <ul class="list-none p-0 m-0 space-y-1">
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Email clients don't auto-play video &mdash; use GIFs, static frames, or click-to-play thumbnails</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">SMS: link to a landing page with the embedded video, not the raw file</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Pull quotes from creator testimonials are the highest-converting email element</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">Video in email is still unreliable &mdash; most email clients (Gmail, Outlook, Apple Mail) will not auto-play embedded video. The workaround is to extract the most compelling 3&ndash;5 seconds as an animated GIF or to use a static thumbnail with a play-button overlay that links to a hosted video page. Keep GIFs under 1&nbsp;MB to avoid clipping in Gmail.</p>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">The highest-performing email derivative is not the video itself &mdash; it is the <strong>pull quote</strong>. Extract the creator's strongest one-liner, pair it with a frame grab showing the creator's face, and use it as a testimonial block in your email template. This converts better than any stock imagery because it is a real person saying something real about your product.</p>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">For SMS, never attach video files directly. SMS has strict file-size limits, and MMS rendering varies wildly across carriers and devices. Instead, link to a landing page that features the embedded UGC video above the fold. Use a short, compelling preview line: &ldquo;See what [Creator] said about [Product] &mdash;&rdquo; followed by the link. The landing page itself becomes another repurposing surface (see section 05).</p>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">Email format checklist</h3>
        <ul class="pl-5 mb-4 space-y-2 text-ink-2">
          <li><strong>GIF preview</strong> &mdash; 3&ndash;5 second loop, under 1&nbsp;MB, links to hosted video</li>
          <li><strong>Static thumbnail</strong> &mdash; creator face + play button overlay, links to hosted video</li>
          <li><strong>Pull-quote block</strong> &mdash; creator's best line + headshot + product name</li>
          <li><strong>Before/after frame</strong> &mdash; two still frames showing the transformation, if applicable</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 05 Product Pages -->
    <section class="guide-section" id="product-pages">
      <div class="max-w-[800px] mx-auto px-6">
        <div class="font-mono text-[11px] font-medium uppercase tracking-[.1em] text-accent mb-2">05 &mdash; Product pages</div>
        <h2 class="font-display font-bold text-[clamp(24px,3.5vw,32px)] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink mb-2">UGC on product pages and landing pages</h2>
        <div class="tldr">
          <ul class="list-none p-0 m-0 space-y-1">
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">UGC testimonial videos on product pages lift conversion rates by 10&ndash;30%</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Place video below the fold, near reviews &mdash; not above the hero image</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Use short clips (15&ndash;30s) with captions; auto-play muted on desktop only</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">Product pages and landing pages are where repurposed UGC has the most direct impact on revenue. A 15&ndash;30 second creator testimonial embedded near the reviews section acts as video social proof &mdash; a real person demonstrating and endorsing the product. Brands that add UGC video to their product pages consistently see a 10&ndash;30% lift in conversion rate compared to pages with only static images and text reviews.</p>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">Placement matters. Do not replace your hero image or product gallery with a UGC video &mdash; shoppers expect professional product shots above the fold. Instead, add the UGC clip below the fold, near customer reviews or in a dedicated &ldquo;See it in action&rdquo; section. On mobile, use a click-to-play thumbnail to avoid layout shift and bandwidth issues. On desktop, auto-play muted with captions can work, but test both approaches.</p>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">For landing pages specifically &mdash; the kind you drive paid traffic to &mdash; the UGC video should be the centerpiece. A full-width embedded video with a creator talking about their experience, followed by bullet-point benefits and a CTA button, is one of the highest-converting landing page formats in DTC e-commerce. This is especially effective when the landing page matches the ad: the viewer clicks on a UGC ad, lands on a page featuring the same creator, and the message continuity drives conversion.</p>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">Implementation tips</h3>
        <ul class="pl-5 mb-4 space-y-2 text-ink-2">
          <li><strong>Load performance</strong> &mdash; host video on a CDN (Cloudflare Stream, Mux, Wistia) rather than self-hosting. A 30-second clip at 720p is typically 8&ndash;15&nbsp;MB; lazy-load it so it does not block initial page render.</li>
          <li><strong>Thumbnail selection</strong> &mdash; choose a frame where the creator's face is clearly visible and expressive. Avoid blurry mid-motion frames. The thumbnail is the first impression on mobile where auto-play may be disabled.</li>
          <li><strong>Caption overlay</strong> &mdash; always burn in captions for product-page embeds. Shoppers browse in public, at work, and in bed with sound off. If the video relies on audio to convey value, most viewers will never hear it.</li>
          <li><strong>Multiple creators</strong> &mdash; if you have UGC from several creators, display two or three clips in a horizontal scroll or tabbed interface. Multiple voices reinforce social proof more than a single testimonial.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 06 Editing Techniques -->
    <section class="guide-section" id="editing">
      <div class="max-w-[800px] mx-auto px-6">
        <div class="font-mono text-[11px] font-medium uppercase tracking-[.1em] text-accent mb-2">06 &mdash; Editing</div>
        <h2 class="font-display font-bold text-[clamp(24px,3.5vw,32px)] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink mb-2">Editing techniques for repurposed content</h2>
        <div class="tldr">
          <ul class="list-none p-0 m-0 space-y-1">
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Always cut from the master asset &mdash; never re-export from a compressed derivative</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Hook swaps, caption overlays, and reframing are the three highest-impact edits</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Preserve the creator's authentic tone &mdash; over-polishing defeats the purpose of UGC</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">The cardinal rule of repurposing: <strong>always work from the master asset</strong>. Every generation of re-export degrades quality. If you crop a TikTok export to square and then re-export that square for email, you have two generations of compression artifacts. Instead, go back to the original file and export directly for each target format.</p>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">Hook swaps</h3>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">The first 3 seconds determine whether the viewer stays or scrolls. Take the body of a proven UGC video (seconds 4&ndash;30) and splice on three to five different opening hooks: a bold statement, a question, a surprising stat, a before/after flash, or a direct address (&ldquo;Hey, if you struggle with&hellip;&rdquo;). Each hook creates a distinct ad variant without any new footage. This is how <a href="/modular-creative" class="text-accent hover:underline">modular creative</a> teams generate testing volume at scale.</p>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">Caption overlays</h3>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">Adding captions is the single most impactful edit for repurposed UGC. Over 80% of social media video is watched without sound initially. Burned-in captions (as opposed to platform-generated auto-captions) give you control over font, size, position, and emphasis. Highlight the key phrase in each sentence with a contrasting color or bold weight. Keep caption blocks to 2&ndash;3 words per line for mobile readability.</p>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">Reframing and safe zones</h3>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">When cropping from 9:16 to 1:1 or 16:9, the subject's face often ends up cut off or off-center. Use keyframed reframing (available in most NLEs and tools like CapCut) to keep the creator's face centered in the new aspect ratio throughout the video. Always check platform safe zones after reframing &mdash; TikTok's UI covers the top 15% and bottom 20%; Instagram Stories cover similar areas. Text and faces must be inside the safe zone.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 07 Rights and Usage -->
    <section class="guide-section" id="rights">
      <div class="max-w-[800px] mx-auto px-6">
        <div class="font-mono text-[11px] font-medium uppercase tracking-[.1em] text-accent mb-2">07 &mdash; Rights &amp; usage</div>
        <h2 class="font-display font-bold text-[clamp(24px,3.5vw,32px)] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink mb-2">Rights and usage: what your contract needs to cover</h2>
        <div class="tldr">
          <ul class="list-none p-0 m-0 space-y-1">
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Your <a href="/ugc-contract" class="text-accent hover:underline">creator contract</a> must explicitly permit derivatives and multi-channel use</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Specify every channel: paid ads, organic social, email, website, OOH, CTV</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Duration matters &mdash; a 90-day license kills your evergreen product-page testimonial</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">Repurposing only works if your usage rights cover every channel you plan to use. Many standard UGC contracts grant rights for &ldquo;paid social advertising&rdquo; &mdash; which does not cover email, product pages, landing pages, CTV, or out-of-home. If your contract says &ldquo;Meta and TikTok ads,&rdquo; you cannot legally use the same content in a Klaviyo email or on your Shopify product page.</p>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">The fix is simple: write your contracts to cover all anticipated uses from the start. Use language like &ldquo;all digital and physical channels including but not limited to paid social advertising, organic social media, email marketing, SMS marketing, website and landing pages, connected TV, display advertising, and out-of-home.&rdquo; Be explicit. Ambiguity benefits neither party.</p>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">Duration and derivatives</h3>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">A 90-day usage license creates a time bomb for repurposed content. If you embed a creator's testimonial on your product page and the license expires, you must pull it down &mdash; losing social proof that was driving conversions. For any content you plan to use on owned channels (website, email templates), negotiate perpetual or extended-term rights. The incremental cost is modest compared to the value of an evergreen asset.</p>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">Your contract should also explicitly grant the right to create <strong>derivative works</strong> &mdash; cropped versions, re-edited cuts, compilations with other creators, still-frame extractions, and audio-only versions. Without this clause, every edit you make is technically a new work that requires separate authorization. See our full <a href="/ugc-contract" class="text-accent hover:underline">UGC contract guide</a> for template language.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 08 Building a Repurposing Workflow -->
    <section class="guide-section" id="workflow">
      <div class="max-w-[800px] mx-auto px-6">
        <div class="font-mono text-[11px] font-medium uppercase tracking-[.1em] text-accent mb-2">08 &mdash; Workflow</div>
        <h2 class="font-display font-bold text-[clamp(24px,3.5vw,32px)] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink mb-2">Building a repurposing workflow</h2>
        <div class="tldr">
          <ul class="list-none p-0 m-0 space-y-1">
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Systematize: every master asset goes through the same repurposing checklist</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Assign roles: editor, media buyer, and email/web team each own their derivatives</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Use a naming convention and asset library &mdash; chaos kills repurposing velocity</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">A repurposing workflow fails without a system. The most common failure mode is ad-hoc: someone remembers to crop a video for email once, forgets the next three times, and the process never becomes consistent. The fix is a checklist that every master asset passes through.</p>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">The repurposing checklist</h3>
        <ol class="list-none p-0 counter-reset-step space-y-0">
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative border-b border-border text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Ingest.</strong> Creator delivers the master asset. Log it in your asset library with metadata: creator name, product, shoot date, usage rights expiry.</li>
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative border-b border-border text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Review.</strong> Watch the full video. Note the strongest hook, the best pull quote, the most expressive moment, and any segments that work as standalone clips.</li>
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative border-b border-border text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Cut.</strong> Editor produces the derivative set: full-length ad, hook tests, short-form, square, landscape, GIF, thumbnail, pull-quote card. Use the ten-derivative framework from section 02.</li>
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative border-b border-border text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Name.</strong> Apply a consistent naming convention. Example: <code class="text-[13px] bg-bg px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded">creator_product_format_length_version</code> &mdash; e.g., <code class="text-[13px] bg-bg px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded">sarah_serum_tiktok_15s_v2</code>.</li>
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative border-b border-border text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Distribute.</strong> Push each derivative to the team that owns that channel: media buyer gets ad cuts, email team gets GIFs and pull quotes, web team gets product-page clips.</li>
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Track.</strong> Log which derivatives were deployed and their performance. Over time, this data reveals which derivative types consistently convert &mdash; and which are not worth producing.</li>
        </ol>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">Tools that help</h3>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">Any NLE (Premiere, DaVinci, CapCut) handles the editing. For asset management, a simple shared Google Drive folder with a consistent naming convention works for small teams. At scale, use a DAM (digital asset management) tool like Air, Brandfolder, or Frame.io. The key is that every team member can find the asset they need in under 30 seconds &mdash; if they cannot, the system is broken.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

